WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
[No sound]

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:07.060
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:28.296
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:28.296 --> 00:00:31.732
[MUSIC - A MEDIEVAL  CHANT]

00:00:31.732 --> 00:00:32.232
 

00:00:32.232 --> 00:00:35.539
THOMAS FORREST KELLY: Welcome to the Houghton Library at Harvard University.

00:00:35.539 --> 00:00:36.955
I'm standing in the entrance hall.

00:00:36.955 --> 00:00:41.800
And behind me is the door to the Reading Room where, with proper introductions,

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:44.990
you can come and look at some of the most beautiful books and manuscripts

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:46.760
and objects you can imagine.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:48.770
There are various exhibition spaces.

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:49.990
I'm standing in one of them.

00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:51.670
There are others upstairs.

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:57.090
But what I'd like you to see with me is an exhibition that a seminar at Harvard

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:03.460
has produced that shows Harvard's collection of medieval scrolls.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:07.180
These are the scrolls that are talked about in this module,

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:09.625
and so this walkthrough of the exhibition

00:01:09.625 --> 00:01:13.120
can serve us as an introduction to medieval scrolls

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:16.070
and to the scrolls in this module.

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:20.150
Or it can serve as a summary of all the things

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:24.420
that you may have looked at in more detail in each of the other units.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:30.470
So in the largest of these exhibition rooms, the Edison and Newman Room,

00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:35.675
we have laid out a collection of almost all of the scrolls

00:01:35.675 --> 00:01:38.000
that belong to Harvard University.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.540
The good news about this whole thing is that it

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:45.600
provides a wonderful representative sampling of the kinds of scrolls that

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.750
survive to us from the Middle Ages.

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:53.140
You'll see some interesting things about scrolls in general in this exhibition.

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:55.450
For one thing, a display case of this size

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:59.020
isn't the perfect way to display something that's 30 feet long.

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:02.490
So the majority of these scrolls have to be rolled up

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:05.540
at the ends-- which, of course, is how the scrolls are mostly

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:07.220
used when they're long.

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:10.190
Nevertheless, it makes it hard to examine the whole thing,

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:13.300
so it's made an interesting choice for us.

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:15.710
We've had to pick what part of each scroll

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:18.380
should be shown to the people who go through.

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:23.780
You lucky people have the opportunity to examine all the surfaces of all

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:27.650
these scrolls, because they've all been very carefully digitized

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:30.370
by the Harvard libraries.

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:32.740
There's a number of things that jump to the eye

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:36.990
when you see these things in the flesh-- in the parchment, as it were.

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:40.420
One is that they vary enormously in size.

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:43.640
On a computer screen, everything's about the same size.

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:46.750
But look at this great big scroll here.

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:48.710
This is a Universal Chronicle.

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:54.090
This is the only one that has had to be displayed sideways, because it's

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:57.070
wider than the display case itself.

00:02:57.070 --> 00:02:59.810
But it's very handsome and very wide.

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:02.070
This is the biggest of the scrolls we have here.

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:09.090
And on the wall behind it is this tiny little poetical scroll

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:13.050
that looks-- you can see to look at it, even in its frame--

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:14.580
that it was once folded.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:19.640
But it seems originally to have been a scroll and is a poetical dialogue.

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:23.570
So very large to very small, but there are other things

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:28.380
that it's possible to notice too-- like qualities of parchment.

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:32.920
Look at this handsome, white parchment here.

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:36.690
And some of the others, you'll see much darker, very beat up.

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:40.970
We also go from scrolls that are quite simple-- this

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:45.310
is a scroll in Greek with very little decoration-- to scrolls

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:49.060
that are very highly decorated.

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:54.520
This is a genealogy of English kings that has almost more pictorial elements

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:55.820
then text.

00:03:55.820 --> 00:04:00.190
To this very beautifully decorated scroll

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:02.870
of prayers with decoration around the top

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:07.350
and the left-hand side, almost as though it were the left-hand page in a codex.

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:11.050
You kind of wonder why there's not decoration down the right hand margin,

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:14.200
and I think it's because the decorator knows how you do a decoration.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:16.279
You go on the outer margin and the top.

00:04:16.279 --> 00:04:18.130
But nobody'll know for sure.

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:21.970
And then there's this scroll here-- this very sad scroll.

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:23.820
This is a chronicle.

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:28.230
And you can see that it once had very beautiful pictures which are now faded

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:33.540
and that some great scholar, no doubt wanting to improve human knowledge,

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:35.960
treated the text of this thing-- hoping that it

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:37.700
would be possible to read it better.

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:41.320
And the result has been to make a hideous mass of the parchment

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:45.010
and of what was once a very, very beautiful scroll.

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:51.270
So the variety of things that we see here and the difference in types

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:56.180
of parchment, size of scroll, level of decoration, level of use

00:04:56.180 --> 00:04:58.430
and where-- all those things are something

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:02.200
that you can only see when you're walking through this exhibition

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:06.010
and looking, almost touching, these very beautiful objects.

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:12.820
In this module, we talk about four kinds of scroll-- not really kinds of scroll,

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:17.500
but four reasons for making a scroll in the Middle Ages

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:22.090
when the standard way of conveying a text was in a book, in a codex.

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:28.180
So I think we have in a way, examples of almost all of those kinds of scroll

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:29.560
in this exhibition.

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:31.780
And looking at them in those categories will

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:36.210
allow us either to introduce or to summarise the other, more detailed,

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:38.120
units that we've had.

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:40.640
One reason for making a scroll is when you

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:44.800
don't know how long the scroll is going to be until you're finished.

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:48.790
One that's a little bit like that is this scroll over here.

00:05:48.790 --> 00:05:51.120
This scroll is a legal scroll, and in a way,

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:53.420
it might be thought of as the kind that could be

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:57.790
extended as more information is needed.

00:05:57.790 --> 00:06:00.700
In fact it's a copy of a treatise-- a treatise by a man

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:03.260
named Glanvill about how to run a court, how

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:05.470
to produce writs and various kinds of things.

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:08.330
And it is in the style of scroll that in England was

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:11.130
called a chancery roll-- that is, one that's

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:13.190
made, like all these other rolls, by making it

00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:16.690
longer by adding an additional piece of parchment to the bottom of it.

00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:20.010
The distinction was made in England because other kinds of scrolls--

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:23.480
and this group of scrolls here is sort of the other way of making rolls.

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:26.130
These are manor court rolls.

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:29.310
They are rolls that record the legal actions

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:32.620
in the courts of English manors-- because the lord of the manor

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:37.250
was officially a judge, and his judgment was recorded in rolls.

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:39.850
This kind of roll, called exchequer rolls,

00:06:39.850 --> 00:06:43.120
is made by stacking the parchment up on top of each other

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:45.850
and binding it together across the top-- rather

00:06:45.850 --> 00:06:50.820
like a flipchart in a classroom-- and then rolling them up.

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:53.374
So big ones of these are sometimes called pipe rolls,

00:06:53.374 --> 00:06:55.040
because they looked sort of like a pipe.

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:58.860
So it's a very different kind of roll from any of the other rolls

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:02.070
we'll see here of this particular kind of scroll.

00:07:02.070 --> 00:07:04.700
So it could get longer as you like, but you do it

00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:07.800
by stacking the pages up rather than adding them to the bottom.

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:10.080
And so it's not really a roll.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:14.950
It's really a codex-- except for the fact that they were stored rolled up.

00:07:14.950 --> 00:07:20.490
So one kind of scroll is the kind whose length is not known.

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:24.440
Another reason for making a scroll is when you want it to be portable,

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:27.480
when you want to carry it around from one place to another.

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:33.310
And this little scroll on the wall here is an example of that kind of thing.

00:07:33.310 --> 00:07:35.535
This is the smallest scroll at Harvard.

00:07:35.535 --> 00:07:37.515
It may not be the smallest medieval scroll

00:07:37.515 --> 00:07:40.150
there is, because there were lots of other times

00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:42.150
when you wanted something very small.

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:45.400
You might want a life of Saint Margaret to wear around your neck.

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:48.090
You might want to have a little scroll of prayers

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:50.860
that you carry with you as a kind of an amulet.

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:53.620
This one is actually a poetic dialogue.

00:07:53.620 --> 00:07:55.500
It's a fairly important text.

00:07:55.500 --> 00:08:00.180
It's a dialogue between Ganymede and Helen about whether the love of man

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:03.980
or the love of women is better.

00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:09.690
And you can see along the sides, the different characters as they speak.

00:08:09.690 --> 00:08:11.820
It's a scroll that once was folded but probably was

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:17.210
intended to be rolled and held in the hand while reciting.

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:21.540
We know that actors very often had a roll.

00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:24.450
Actors still play roles, and the reason what actors do

00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:29.110
is called a role is because they held their part on a roll in their hand.

00:08:29.110 --> 00:08:33.179
This is not exactly an actor's roll, but it's the kind of very small thing

00:08:33.179 --> 00:08:36.010
that poets are often depicted as holding in their hands

00:08:36.010 --> 00:08:38.049
and that we know actors did.

00:08:38.049 --> 00:08:42.260
So the idea of having it be small so it can be portable.

00:08:42.260 --> 00:08:44.730
There are other kinds of portable scrolls, not all of which

00:08:44.730 --> 00:08:46.920
are illustrated, in this exhibition.

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:49.230
Sometimes processional music for churches,

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:52.720
you don't want to carry the whole, great big gradual around with you.

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:55.150
So you write the necessary stuff on a scroll

00:08:55.150 --> 00:08:58.560
to go carry around and bless the crops or bless

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:00.570
the different churches of the city.

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:03.880
Across the way here is another scroll that we might think

00:09:03.880 --> 00:09:06.200
of as being in the portable category.

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:13.240
It's a series of prayers that are designed

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:16.200
to provide you time off from purgatory.

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:20.230
This is a very beautiful scroll, and you can imagine that if it were rolled up,

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:22.330
it would be a little bit smaller than this.

00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:24.410
So it might be a nice thing to carry around.

00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:27.242
It might once also have been hung on the wall or down low for you

00:09:27.242 --> 00:09:28.700
to kneel down and pray.

00:09:28.700 --> 00:09:32.200
But if you say all these prayers-- you see that they almost all begin with

00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:33.050
&amp;quot;O.&amp;quot;

00:09:33.050 --> 00:09:35.790
If you say all these prayers, you get x number

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:38.710
of hundreds of years off in purgatory.

00:09:38.710 --> 00:09:40.050
You can see this scroll in here.

00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:43.780
It was discussed in one of the other units.

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:46.640
But it seems clear that it's small so that it

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:51.290
can be carried around, because it's an object of personal devotion.

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:56.246
 

